$body = <<<'HTML'
<div class="takeaways">
  <p class="takeaways__title">How long metal roofs last — the short answer</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A well-installed residential metal roof typically lasts 40–70 years; copper and zinc can last much longer.</li>
    <li>Standing seam roofs with concealed fasteners generally outlast exposed-fastener panels, whose screws and washers need periodic attention.</li>
    <li>Stone-coated steel combines a steel core with a granular finish and commonly lasts 40–70 years, with better hail tolerance than bare painted panels.</li>
    <li>The finish (PVDF vs polyester/SMP), metal gauge, underlayment and installation details decide whether a roof reaches the top or bottom of its range.</li>
    <li>In Central Texas, heat cycling and hail are the main stresses — not rust.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<p class="lead">Residential metal roofing lifespan is one of the biggest reasons homeowners switch from shingles. Most metal roofs last two to three times as long as asphalt shingles, but “metal” covers many products. This guide explains how long each type of metal roof lasts, the profile and coating choices that affect longevity, and what Central Texas heat and hail mean for durability.</p>

<p>At Austin Roofing we install and repair standing seam, exposed-fastener and stone-coated steel roofs across the Austin metro. The numbers below are typical industry ranges for properly installed systems; individual results depend on product, installation and exposure.</p>

<h2 id="h-what-is-the-average-lifespan-of-a-residential-metal-roof">What Is the Average Lifespan of a Residential Metal Roof?</h2>
<p>Across products, a residential metal roof generally lasts <strong>40 to 70 years</strong>. Lower-cost exposed-fastener steel panels sit at the lower end, while premium standing seam steel and aluminum reach the higher end. Specialty metals like copper and zinc can exceed a century in the right conditions.</p>
<p>For comparison, asphalt shingles in Central Texas typically last about 15–30 years depending on the product. That means many metal roofs will see a homeowner through several shingle cycles. If you want to estimate your current roof’s remaining life, try our <a href="/roofing-tools/roof-lifespan-calculator/">roof lifespan calculator</a>.</p>

<h2 id="h-types-of-metal-roofing-materials-and-their-lifespan">Types of Metal Roofing Materials and Their Lifespan</h2>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Metal</th><th>Typical residential lifespan</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Galvanized steel (zinc-coated)</td><td>20–40 years</td><td>Lower cost; longevity depends on coating weight and finish</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Galvalume steel (aluminum-zinc coated)</td><td>40–60+ years</td><td>The most common residential metal roofing substrate</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Stone-coated steel</td><td>40–70 years</td><td>Steel core with acrylic-bonded stone granules; shingle, shake or tile look</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Aluminum</td><td>40–60+ years</td><td>Will not rust; preferred near salt water; softer and dents more easily</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Copper</td><td>70–100+ years</td><td>Develops a patina; premium cost; often used on accents</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Zinc</td><td>60–100+ years</td><td>Self-healing patina; specialty installation</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p><em>Typical industry ranges for properly installed and maintained roofs.</em></p>

<h3 id="h-stone-coated-steel">Stone-coated steel</h3>
<p>Stone-coated steel deserves a closer look for Austin homeowners. Panels are formed from coated steel and finished with stone granules bonded in acrylic, so the roof can mimic shingles, shake or clay tile while weighing far less than tile. The granular surface hides minor dents and helps the roof tolerate hail better than bare painted panels — many stone-coated products carry Class 4 impact ratings. Manufacturers commonly offer long warranties, and lifespans of 40–70 years are typical. Repairs require matching panels and experience with the system, so look for a contractor who works on stone-coated steel regularly.</p>

<h2 id="h-how-roofing-profiles-affect-longevity">How Roofing Profiles Affect Longevity</h2>
<p>The panel profile — how the panels connect and fasten — matters as much as the metal.</p>
<div class="feature-grid">
  <div class="feature"><h3>Standing seam (concealed fastener)</h3><p>Raised seams lock panels together, and clips hidden under the seams hold them to the deck. No exposed screws, and clips allow thermal movement. Typically 50–70 years.</p></div>
  <div class="feature"><h3>Exposed-fastener panels</h3><p>Corrugated or ribbed panels screwed through the face. Lower cost, but screw washers degrade in UV and heat and may need replacement every 10–20 years. Typically 20–40 years.</p></div>
  <div class="feature"><h3>Metal shingles and stone-coated panels</h3><p>Interlocking modular panels that mimic other materials. Typically 40–70 years.</p></div>
</div>
<p>In Central Texas, the daily temperature swing on a roof can be large, and metal expands and contracts with it. Standing seam clip systems are designed for that movement. On exposed-fastener roofs, that same cycling slowly works screws loose and wallows out holes — the most common leak source we see on older metal roofs.</p>

<h2 id="h-the-role-of-protective-coatings-and-finishes">The Role of Protective Coatings and Finishes</h2>
<p>Steel roofing relies on layered protection: a metallic coating (zinc or aluminum-zinc) against corrosion, then primer and paint. The paint system largely determines how the roof looks over time.</p>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li><strong>PVDF (often sold under the Kynar 500 name):</strong> premium resin with excellent color and gloss retention; finish warranties of 30+ years are common.</li>
  <li><strong>SMP (silicone-modified polyester):</strong> good mid-range finish, more fading and chalking than PVDF over time.</li>
  <li><strong>Polyester:</strong> economy finish with shorter fade and chalk protection.</li>
</ul>
<p>Under Texas sun, the finish choice is not just cosmetic — a durable finish keeps the metallic coating protected longer. Reflective “cool roof” pigments also keep panels cooler, which reduces thermal stress.</p>

<h2 id="h-environmental-factors-that-influence-metal-roof-longevity">Environmental Factors That Influence Metal Roof Longevity in Central Texas</h2>
<div class="feature-grid">
  <div class="feature"><h3>Heat and UV</h3><p>Long, intense summers fade finishes and drive thermal movement. Lighter, reflective colors age more gracefully.</p></div>
  <div class="feature"><h3>Hail</h3><p>Large hail can dent panels. Most dents do not cause leaks, but they can affect appearance and, in some cases, the coating.</p></div>
  <div class="feature"><h3>Wind</h3><p>Properly fastened metal roofs resist high winds well; failures usually start at edges, ridges and trim.</p></div>
  <div class="feature"><h3>Trees and debris</h3><p>Oak leaves and cedar debris trap moisture against panels and in valleys, encouraging corrosion over time.</p></div>
</div>
<p>Unlike coastal Texas, Austin does not face salt-air corrosion, so steel roofs generally age well here. One insurance note: many Texas policies now carry cosmetic damage exclusions for metal roofs, meaning hail dents that do not cause leaks may not be covered. Our guide to <a href="/blog/insurance-coverage-for-storm-damaged-roofs/">insurance coverage for storm-damaged roofs</a> explains how that works.</p>

<h3 id="h-hoa-and-neighborhood-considerations">HOA and neighborhood considerations around Austin</h3>
<p>Before committing to metal, check your HOA guidelines. Some Austin-area associations restrict exposed-fastener panels or limit colors, while stone-coated steel often satisfies rules written for shingle or tile appearance. Texas law limits how far many HOAs can go in banning certain energy-efficient or impact-resistant roofing, but associations can usually still regulate appearance, so submit product samples and color names for written approval. Older homes in central neighborhoods may also have historic review requirements if the roof’s look changes significantly. Getting approvals sorted early keeps the project on schedule and prevents costly changes later.</p>

<h2 id="h-installation-quality-and-its-impact-on-lifespan">Installation Quality and Its Impact on Lifespan</h2>
<p>Even the best panel fails early if it is installed poorly. Details that make or break longevity:</p>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><h3>Correct underlayment</h3><p>Metal needs a high-temperature underlayment rated for the heat beneath panels. See our comparison of <a href="/blog/roofing-underlayment-material-types/">roof underlayment types</a>.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Allowance for movement</h3><p>Clips, slotted holes and properly spaced fasteners let panels expand and contract.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Compatible metals</h3><p>Mixing dissimilar metals (for example, copper and galvanized steel) can cause galvanic corrosion. Fasteners, flashing and trim must be compatible.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Clean cuts and trim</h3><p>Cutting panels with abrasive saws can burn coatings and leave hot filings that rust. Proper shears and cleanup matter.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Flashing details</h3><p>Pipe boots, chimneys, skylights and wall transitions are where leaks start; they need metal-specific flashing.</p></li>
</ol>

<h2 id="h-maintenance-requirements-for-metal-roofing">Maintenance Requirements for Metal Roofing</h2>
<p>Metal roofs are low-maintenance, not no-maintenance. A simple routine extends life:</p>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li>Inspect once a year and after major storms — a <a href="/services/roof-inspection/">professional roof inspection</a> includes photos.</li>
  <li>Check exposed fasteners for backed-out screws and cracked washers.</li>
  <li>Clear leaves and debris from valleys, gutters and behind chimneys.</li>
  <li>Look for scratches or cut edges where bare metal is exposed, and touch them up.</li>
  <li>Check sealants at flashing and pipe boots, which age faster than the panels.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="h-metal-gauge-and-thickness">Metal Gauge and Thickness</h2>
<p>Steel roofing thickness is measured in gauge, where a lower number means thicker metal. Residential panels are commonly 29, 26 or 24 gauge. Thinner 29-gauge panels are typical of budget exposed-fastener products, while 24 and 26 gauge are common for standing seam. Thicker metal resists denting from hail and foot traffic, spans better between supports and stays flatter (less “oil canning,” the visible waviness in flat panel areas). Aluminum is measured differently, usually in thousandths of an inch, and needs to be thicker than steel for the same stiffness.</p>
<p>For a home in hail-prone parts of Williamson or Travis County, stepping up a gauge is one of the most cost-effective ways to improve how the roof holds up over decades.</p>

<h2 id="h-signs-a-metal-roof-is-wearing-out">Signs a Metal Roof Is Nearing the End of Its Life</h2>
<p>Metal roofs fail slowly, which gives you time to plan. Watch for:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Rust that spreads</strong> from cut edges, scratches or fastener holes into the field of the panel</li>
  <li><strong>Heavy chalking and fading</strong> that shows the paint system has broken down</li>
  <li><strong>Repeated leaks at fasteners</strong> on exposed-fastener roofs, even after screws are replaced</li>
  <li><strong>Loose or lifting seams and trim</strong>, especially along ridges and eaves after wind events</li>
  <li><strong>Widespread hail damage</strong> that has cracked the coating and exposed bare metal</li>
  <li><strong>Soft decking or attic moisture</strong> that points to long-term leaks or condensation</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="h-repairing-vs-replacing-a-metal-roof">Repairing vs Replacing a Metal Roof</h2>
<p>Many metal roof problems are repairable. Fastener replacement, resealing flashing, replacing a damaged panel or adding new trim can add years of service. On older exposed-fastener roofs with sound panels, a restoration coating system may be an option to extend life further. Replacement makes more sense when corrosion is widespread, panels are thin and heavily dented, or leaks keep recurring across the roof.</p>
<p>Because metal roofs last so long, decisions about repair or replacement tend to come once in a homeowner’s tenure. A careful inspection — with photos of fasteners, seams and flashing — is the best way to decide.</p>

<h2 id="h-can-a-metal-roof-outlast-the-home">Can a Metal Roof Outlast the Home?</h2>
<p>For many homeowners, yes — a standing seam or stone-coated steel roof installed in your 40s may be the last roof you buy. Some homes keep their original metal roofs for generations. Whether yours does depends on the material, finish and quality of installation, plus how long you stay in the home.</p>

<h2 id="h-energy-efficiency-and-its-relationship-to-longevity">Energy Efficiency and Its Relationship to Longevity</h2>
<p>Metal roofs reflect a larger share of solar energy than dark shingles, especially in light colors or with reflective pigments. That lowers attic temperatures and can reduce cooling costs during long Austin summers. Cooler panels also experience less thermal stress, which supports a longer life. Pair a metal roof with balanced attic ventilation for the best results.</p>

<h2 id="h-comparing-metal-roofing-lifespan-to-other-materials">Comparing Metal Roofing Lifespan to Other Materials</h2>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Roofing material</th><th>Typical lifespan in Central Texas</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>3-tab asphalt shingles</td><td>15–20 years</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Architectural asphalt shingles</td><td>20–30 years</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Exposed-fastener metal</td><td>20–40 years</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Standing seam metal</td><td>50–70 years</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Stone-coated steel</td><td>40–70 years</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Concrete or clay tile</td><td>50+ years (underlayment may need earlier replacement)</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>See how shingle options compare in our <a href="/blog/architectural-shingle-vs-asphalt-shingles/">architectural vs 3-tab shingles</a> guide.</p>

<h2 id="h-is-residential-metal-roofing-worth-it-for-long-term-homeowners">Is Residential Metal Roofing Worth It for Long-Term Homeowners?</h2>
<p>Metal roofs cost more upfront than shingles — often two to three times as much installed, depending on the system. The value comes from time: if you plan to stay 15 years or more, a metal roof can cost less per year than two or three shingle replacements. Other benefits include strong wind performance, fire resistance, recyclability and lower cooling loads.</p>
<div class="pros-cons">
  <div class="pros"><h4>Pros</h4><ul><li>40–70+ year lifespan</li><li>Excellent wind and fire resistance</li><li>Reflective finishes reduce heat gain</li><li>Lightweight compared with tile</li><li>Recyclable at end of life</li></ul></div>
  <div class="cons"><h4>Cons</h4><ul><li>Higher upfront cost</li><li>Can dent in large hail; cosmetic exclusions may apply</li><li>Requires experienced installers</li><li>Repairs need matching panels and trim</li></ul></div>
</div>

<h2 id="h-final-thoughts-on-residential-metal-roofing-lifespan">Final Thoughts on Residential Metal Roofing Lifespan</h2>
<p>Residential metal roofing lifespan depends on the metal, the profile, the finish and the installation. For most Central Texas homes, Galvalume standing seam with a PVDF finish or a stone-coated steel system offers an excellent balance of longevity and value. To explore options for your home, visit our <a href="/services/metal-roofing/">Austin metal roofing</a> page or <a href="/contact-us/">contact Austin Roofing</a> for a free inspection and no-pressure quote.</p>
